webdesk is MIT licensed. It is built on third-party components that keep
their own licenses, which the Docker images distribute alongside it.

Permissive, nothing to do beyond keeping their notices:

  fastapi, pydantic          MIT
  uvicorn, starlette         BSD-3-Clause
  cryptography               Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause
  pion/webrtc and its deps   MIT
  jezek/xgb                  BSD-3-Clause
  golang.org/x/*             BSD-3-Clause

Worth knowing:

  asyncssh                   EPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later

  The backend imports asyncssh and the backend image ships it. Under the
  EPL-2.0 option, a larger work may be distributed under other terms --
  webdesk's own code stays MIT -- while asyncssh itself remains EPL-2.0
  and its source has to stay available. It is used as published, with no
  modifications.

  ffmpeg is not distributed here. The host program runs whatever ffmpeg is
  already installed on the machine being controlled. The local test image
  in test/ installs it for development only and is not published.
